The two items in the header marked NOT VERIFIED are here, first, because the
change is not correct until they are settled. The rest follows.

  1. THE HANDLER'S RETURN CONTRACT. Open webhook.py and read what
     handle_update returns. Reconcile status_for with it. If the handler
     signals by raising rather than returning, replace status_for with the
     matching exception handling and keep the fail-closed default. An
     unrecognized answer must remain a refusal.

  2. WHERE THE THREE GUARDS ACTUALLY LIVE. The documentation says the relay
     performs a constant-time comparison, an allowlist check and an update
     dedup, and that handle_update is the transport-independent core. It does
     not say all three are inside handle_update. Read the stdlib request
     handler. If any of the three lives in the handler rather than in
     handle_update, then STOP: move it into handle_update, or call it from the
     ASGI route before handle_update, before this change ships. A wrapper that
     leaves a guard behind in the class it replaced is the regression this
     specification is most likely to cause.

  3. THE IMPORT SURFACE. Confirm that "from webhook import handle_update"
     resolves in the target package layout. If the relay is a package rather
     than a flat module directory, adjust the import and the two reload calls
